(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 52-year-old woman and her 52-year-old husband ask at a wellness visit whether their age already counts as a coronary risk factor. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["For both of them","Only for her husband","Only for her","For neither of them"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age becomes a nonmodifiable coronary risk factor at 45 or older for men and 55 or older for women. At 52 he has crossed his threshold and she has not. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man lost consciousness at work. Coworkers saw rhythmic jerking of all four limbs, he bit his tongue and was incontinent, and he remained confused for 30 minutes. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["A seizure","Situational reflex syncope","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tongue biting, incontinence and prolonged confusion afterward point to seizure, a non-hypotensive cause; syncope ends in rapid, spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy with confirmed homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has total cholesterol of 610 mg/dL on a high-intensity statin with ezetimibe. What is the treatment of choice for his disorder?","choices":["Add a fibrate for his triglyceride","Very low-fat diet alone for a year","Switch to a moderate-intensity statin","Low-density lipoprotein apheresis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis is the treatment of choice, like dialysis: the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation. Most patients also need several drugs, starting with high-intensity statins. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has had diarrhea and poor oral intake for 3 days and fainted when he stood up quickly this morning. His oral mucous membranes are dry. Supine he is 134/76 with a heart rate of 66; standing he is 104/62 with a heart rate of 94. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Situational reflex syncope","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Postural orthostatic tachycardia","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A 30 mmHg systolic fall with a heart rate rise from 66 to 94 shows an intact sympathetic reflex, and diarrhea with poor intake explains the volume depletion. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with known mitral valve prolapse is followed over several years. Which complication can the prolapse progress to?","choices":["Aortic stenosis","Tricuspid stenosis","Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse may develop the signs and symptoms of mitral regurgitation; prolapse is also listed among the chronic causes of mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 54, 45"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with mildly reduced ejection fraction (fraction 44%) has ankle edema and breathlessness. Which therapy carries the strongest recommendation?","choices":["A neprilysin combination drug","A diuretic as needed","An evidence-based beta blocker","A mineralocorticoid antagonist"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In mildly reduced ejection fraction, diuretics as needed carry the one strong recommendation. The sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor is moderate; the beta blocker, antagonist and renin-angiotensin drugs are weak. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Why is a second troponin drawn after the first?","choices":["To watch for a rise","To confirm the units","To replace the stress test","To grade the angina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When you get one, you have to get two: the change between draws is what matters, and a rising second value leads to a third as more cells die. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with chest pain in the emergency department is being risk-stratified with the HEART score. Which element does this score include?","choices":["Chest radiograph","Initial troponin","D-dimer level","Calcium score"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The HEART score is built from History, Electrocardiogram, Age, Risk factors and the initial Troponin. It predicts the risk of an adverse outcome in acute coronary syndrome. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is recovering from an ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Several weeks later he develops pleuritic chest pain and a pericardial friction rub. Which complication of the infarction does this represent?","choices":["Thromboembolism","Pericarditis","Myocardial rupture","Ventricular aneurysm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis (Dressler syndrome) is listed among the mechanical complications of myocardial infarction. Pleuritic pain with a friction rub weeks after the infarct points to the pericardium rather than the ventricle. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with borderline-high low-density lipoprotein asks which foods can help lower his cholesterol. Which dietary advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Cut out all vegetables","Add more saturated and trans fat","Add soluble fiber and plant sterols","Eat more refined carbohydrate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Therapeutic lifestyle changes include less saturated fat, more soluble fiber and plant stanols or sterols, cholesterol-lowering substances found in grains, vegetables, fruits, nuts and seeds. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 22%) is breathless even at rest. He does not yet take a renin-angiotensin drug. Which renin-angiotensin option fits his symptom class?","choices":["A converting enzyme inhibitor or receptor blocker","No renin-angiotensin drug once class IV","An angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor","A calcium channel blocker such as verapamil"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms at rest are class IV. An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or receptor blocker covers class II to IV, while the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor is recommended for class II to III. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which description defines syncope?","choices":["Brief loss of consciousness, then full recovery","Collapse with confusion lasting an hour","Dizziness and tunnel vision without collapse","Prolonged unconsciousness needing support"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Syncope is a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness with inability to maintain postural tone, followed by spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with newly diagnosed hypertension has proximal muscle weakness, easy bruising, thin skin and broad purple abdominal striae with central adiposity. What is the most likely underlying cause?","choices":["Cushing syndrome","Primary aldosteronism","Obstructive sleep apnea","Pheochromocytoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cluster of proximal weakness, bruising, thin skin, broad purple striae and central adiposity points to glucocorticoid excess as the secondary cause of her hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with reduced ejection fraction is started on spironolactone alongside her loop diuretic. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["It replaces the need for her beta blocker","It spares potassium, so levels are checked","It wastes potassium like her loop diuretic","It is only a short course for congestion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spironolactone is a mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist, a potassium-sparing diuretic, which is why her potassium and renal function will be followed alongside the potassium-wasting loop. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"When should primary aldosteronism be screened for?","choices":["Resistant pressure with unexplained hypokalemia","Every newly diagnosed hypertensive patient","Only in patients with a known adrenal tumor","Only after three drugs have failed and sleep apnea is excluded"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Each is a departure from the ordinary pattern, and together they define a group in which a single morning blood test can change the whole direction of management. An adrenal incidentaloma and early or familial hypertension are the other triggers. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman reports six months of breathlessness when she walks her dog and one episode of fainting while gardening. She has never had cardiac imaging. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Left heart catheterization","Electrocardiogram alone","Transesophageal echocardiogram","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Any patient with symptoms suggestive of aortic stenosis (exertional chest pressure, shortness of breath or syncope) should get an echocardiogram. Transthoracic imaging shows thickened, calcified leaflets with reduced opening. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 18, 22"},{"q":"Why can nitroglycerin cause shock in a right ventricular infarct?","choices":["It blocks the atrioventricular node","It provokes coronary spasm","Lost preload drops output","It triggers major bleeding"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The infarcted right ventricle is preload dependent; decreasing preload reduces the Frank-Starling effect, the ventricle fails into right-sided heart failure, output falls and shock follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a surgical valvuloplasty as a child to relieve right ventricular outflow obstruction. He now has a brief, high-pitched decrescendo early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space that radiates toward the mid right sternal border with full inspiration. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonic regurgitation","Pulmonic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A brief, high-pitched early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space with inspiratory radiation (the Graham Steell murmur) is pulmonic regurgitation, and surgery on the right ventricular outflow tract is the most common cause of significant regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 63, 61"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has weight loss, tremor, heat intolerance and palpitations for two months, and now ankle edema and breathlessness. Heart rate is 124 and B-type natriuretic peptide is elevated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Right-sided failure from mitral stenosis","Systolic failure from ischemic disease","High-output failure from hyperthyroidism","Diastolic failure from hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hyperthyroidism raises peripheral demand, and the heart answers with a faster rate and stronger contraction until the heart failure syndrome appears: fatigue, dyspnea, edema and palpitations. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the usual cause of pulmonic stenosis?","choices":["Intravenous drug use","Congenital disease","Rheumatic fever","Degenerative calcification"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis, obstruction of right ventricular outflow to the lungs, is almost always congenital. Carcinoid disease is another cause, and it is commonly part of tetralogy of Fallot. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 53-year-old woman with severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is scheduled for valve replacement. Which valve type is preferred?","choices":["Bioprosthetic valve","Annuloplasty ring","Mechanical valve","Balloon valvotomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis, bioprosthetic valve replacement is preferred; balloon valvotomy and repair are reserved for hepatic congestion or severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has recurrent anginal chest pain during periods of work stress and anxiety. Her electrocardiogram shows ST changes during symptoms, and coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unstable angina","Vasospastic angina","Stable angina","Microvascular angina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina (formerly cardiac syndrome X) is angina with ST changes, possibly with elevated biomarkers, but normal coronary arteries on the angiogram; it is more frequent in women and often associated with stress or anxiety. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 93, 94"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has just recovered from replacement of his aortic valve with a mechanical valve. Which long-term antithrombotic therapy is recommended?","choices":["No therapy after the recovery period","Heparin as permanent outpatient therapy","Aspirin alone as antiplatelet therapy","Warfarin, a vitamin K antagonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In patients with mechanical heart valves, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended for long-term management over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents. Warfarin is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 21"},{"q":"For a patient at risk for heart failure who has diabetes, which drug class is recommended first?","choices":["Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Beta blocker as the first agent","Loop diuretic such as furosemide","Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In stage A, at risk for heart failure, the one strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. A beta blocker enters only at stage B, once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which is a coronary heart disease risk equivalent?","choices":["Isolated high-density lipoprotein of 65","Borderline triglyceride of 170","Low dietary fiber","Diabetes mellitus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Coronary risk equivalents include diabetes mellitus, peripheral arterial disease, abdominal aortic aneurysm, symptomatic carotid disease and a Framingham risk above 20%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has progressive symptoms despite treatment of its cause and is not a transplant candidate. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Discharge without follow-up care","Calcium channel blocker for comfort","Inotropes started for home use","Palliative care focused on symptoms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Right-sided failure is treated like left-sided failure, with symptom management, treatment of the cause, heart transplant or palliative care. When transplant is not an option, palliative care is the route. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman consistently reads 150/92 in clinic but her seven-day home average is 118/74 with a validated upper-arm device and correct technique. Which pattern does this represent?","choices":["White coat hypertension","Masked hypertension","Resistant hypertension","Stage 2 hypertension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: raised office readings with normal out-of-office readings. Recognizing it avoids treating a patient whose true pressure is normal. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is normal cardiac output?","choices":["15 to 20 liters per minute","5 to 6 liters per minute","10 to 12 liters per minute","1 to 2 liters per minute"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normal cardiac output is 5 to 6 liters per minute, the product of stroke volume and heart rate. It is a favorite board fact and distinct from ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 140 mg/dL and a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 10%. She is unsure whether to start a statin, and no risk enhancers are present. Which test can help the decision?","choices":["Skin biopsy for receptor activity","Coronary artery calcium score","Repeat total cholesterol","Fundoscopic examination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In adults 40 to 75 without diabetes, with low-density lipoprotein of 70 to 189 and 10-year risk of 7.5% to 19.9%, coronary artery calcium scoring is considered when the statin decision is uncertain. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman is started on chlorthalidone. Which monitoring matters most for her?","choices":["Liver enzymes every three months","Sodium, given the risk of thiazide hyponatremia","No monitoring, since chlorthalidone is well tolerated","Thyroid function every six months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, particularly where sodium is already low-normal, and volume status matters in older adults too. Electrolytes and uric acid are the standing monitoring for the class. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 66-year-old with confirmed renal artery stenosis has recurrent flash pulmonary edema and progressive renal decline despite optimal medical therapy. What is the appropriate step?","choices":["Add a second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker","Continue medical therapy; revascularization is never indicated","Refer any patient in whom screening finds renal artery narrowing","Early referral for revascularization assessment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent flash pulmonary edema, progressive renal decline despite optimal therapy, and truly refractory control are the three features that trigger early referral, rather than the mere presence of narrowing on imaging. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with chronic heart failure has increasing lethargy, episodes of near-syncope and cool extremities, without new edema. Which feature of the heart failure syndrome do these findings reflect?","choices":["Worsening pulmonary congestion","Portal venous overload","Inadequate tissue perfusion","Systemic volume overload"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Weakness, fatigue, lethargy, malaise, syncope or near-syncope and obtundation are signs of inadequate tissue perfusion, the low-output side of the syndrome, distinct from congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 43-year-old man has a fasting triglyceride of 620 mg/dL. He drinks heavily most nights. Before any drug is started, which additional conditions should be evaluated?","choices":["Tendon xanthomas and corneal arcus","Diabetes, kidney disease, hypothyroidism","Anorexia nervosa and biliary cirrhosis","Lipoprotein(a) and coronary calcium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. With triglyceride of 500 or more rule out diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, alcoholism, pregnancy and hypothyroidism; his alcohol history is important too. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with two prior heart attacks, diabetes and hypertension takes a maximal statin. Her low-density lipoprotein is 95 mg/dL, and she takes no other lipid drug. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add ezetimibe","Add evolocumab","Add niacin","Stop the statin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In very high-risk disease, when low-density lipoprotein stays 70 or more on a maximally tolerated statin, adding ezetimibe is reasonable, and it comes before any injectable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman has grade II diastolic dysfunction with an ejection fraction of 46%. She has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion but has never had pulmonary or peripheral edema and has never needed a diuretic. Does she meet a clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure?","choices":["No, because congestion is required","Yes, because she has symptoms","Yes, because her fraction is 46%","Yes, because she is older than 65"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A clinical diagnosis of congestive heart failure requires congestion, meaning pulmonary or peripheral edema. She has symptoms and a mildly reduced ejection fraction, but without edema the congestive label does not apply. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. She stands up quickly from squatting while the examiner listens. How does the murmur change?","choices":["It gains a click","It does not change","It becomes softer","It becomes louder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mitral regurgitation murmur decreases with standing or the Valsalva maneuver and increases with squatting and handgrip, which raise resistance to ventricular ejection. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with type 2 diabetes, obesity and a normal echocardiogram has no symptoms. His clinician plans to start metoprolol to prevent heart failure. Which drug class should not be chosen first here?","choices":["A beta blocker","Glucose-lowering therapy in general","A sodium-glucose drug such as empagliflozin","Lifestyle risk factor control"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He is stage A, at risk with a normal heart, and the first drug for an at-risk patient with diabetes is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor. The beta blocker is a stage B therapy once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which test confirms the lesion?","choices":["Heart catheterization","Electrocardiogram","Doppler echocardiogram","Chest radiograph"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation is diagnosed with Doppler echocardiography, which shows the regurgitation and may show prolapsed, scarred or displaced leaflets. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with prior heart attacks collapsed without any prodrome while climbing stairs and woke within 20 seconds. Which referral should accompany her cardiac testing?","choices":["Neurology consultation","Physical therapy referral","Dietitian referral","Cardiology consultation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Arrhythmic, structural and vascular cardiogenic syncope each pair their tests with cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman on two agents has an average blood pressure of 134/84. What should happen?","choices":["Stop one agent, since she is close to goal","Accept whatever value she tolerates without symptoms","Accept this as at goal, since it is below 140/90","Intensify therapy, since the goal is below 130/80"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and values closer to 120/80 are encouraged where appropriate. The same threshold defines the condition and measures control. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man had an anterior myocardial infarction one week ago with acute heart failure. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 30%. He is now stable. Which is the most appropriate next step for protection against sudden death?","choices":["Home inotropes until the heart recovers","Implantable defibrillator before discharge","Wearable defibrillator while therapy is optimized","No protection until a repeat echo in a year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After acute heart failure from infarction with an ejection fraction below 35%, the pathway is a wearable defibrillator vest, optimized guideline-directed therapy with cardiac rehabilitation, a repeat echocardiogram, and an implantable device only if there is no improvement. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has 30 minutes of crushing chest pressure. His electrocardiogram shows a new left bundle branch block; a tracing last month was normal. How should this be managed?","choices":["As an ST-elevation infarct","As stable angina","As unstable angina","As a benign conduction delay"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A new left bundle branch block with ischemic symptoms is treated as an ST-elevation myocardial infarction equivalent: aspirin and activation of the catheterization team without waiting. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes has a triglyceride of 720 mg/dL, elevated total cholesterol, reduced high-density lipoprotein and a normal low-density lipoprotein. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mixed dyslipidemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Heterozygous familial form","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 500 with elevated total cholesterol and reduced high-density lipoprotein, usually without raised low-density lipoprotein, defines it, typically on a polygenic background with obesity or insulin resistance. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the commonest cause to exclude before labeling hypertension as resistant?","choices":["Excess alcohol intake","Undiagnosed primary aldosteronism","Incorrect cuff size","Medication nonadherence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list that also includes measurement error, white coat effect, high sodium or alcohol intake, interfering drugs, and undiagnosed sleep apnea or secondary hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man starting home monitoring asks how to do it. What should he be told?","choices":["One reading each morning for three days","Readings only when he feels unwell","Two readings morning and evening for seven days","Continuous monitoring over twenty-four hours at home"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the accompanying instruction matters as much: do not overreact to the highest single reading, because acting on outliers generates anxiety and unnecessary changes in therapy. They are taken at least a minute apart and the average is reported. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with known dilated cardiomyopathy has worsening breathlessness. Blood pressure is 102/64, a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) is present, and he has crackles. Which ejection fraction is the echocardiogram expected to show?","choices":["About 60%","About 45%","About 25%","About 75%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dilated cardiomyopathy is the systolic chain: poor contraction, a reduced ejection fraction of 40% or less, low-normal pressure, a third heart sound and pulmonary edema. A value near 25% fits. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which triggers are typical of vasovagal syncope?","choices":["Urination, coughing or sneezing","Exertion with known heart disease","Fear, pain, heat or prolonged standing","Standing after a diuretic increase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vasovagal syncope is triggered by emotional stress, fear, pain, heat exposure or standing for a long time, and trigger avoidance is its treatment. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which description fits the mitral stenosis murmur?","choices":["Mid-diastolic rumble near the xiphoid that is louder on inspiration","Holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla","Low-pitched diastolic rumble at the apex after an opening snap","High-pitched diastolic blowing murmur at the left upper sternal border"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis is a low-pitched decrescendo-crescendo rumbling diastolic murmur at the apex that starts with an opening snap after the second heart sound. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with heart failure has an echocardiogram reporting an ejection fraction of 44% and a multigated acquisition scan the same month reporting 32%. Which result should decide her ejection fraction group?","choices":["The echocardiogram","An average of the two","Whichever value is higher","The multigated acquisition scan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The multigated acquisition scan is the most accurate study for ejection fraction, so its 32% places her in reduced ejection fraction. The echocardiogram is the one to trust for structures such as the valves. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with exertional chest pain says he \"had a heart cath\" last week. The report shows dye was injected and the coronary arteries were imaged, revealing a stenosis, but nothing was done to the vessel. Which term names what was performed?","choices":["Percutaneous coronary intervention","Coronary artery bypass graft","Coronary angiography","Plain old balloon angioplasty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Coronary angiography is the injection of dye to identify a lesion or blockage, and it is the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease. Nothing was done to the vessel, so no intervention took place. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 50"},{"q":"A 20-year-old woman has a classic history of palpitations and lightheadedness on standing that improve lying down, but her tilt-table test is negative. How should the negative result be interpreted?","choices":["It points to autonomic failure","It points to a cardiac cause","It rules the syndrome out","It does not exclude the syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a sensitivity of about 40%, many patients with the syndrome test negative, so a negative result tells almost nothing; a positive result, with specificity above 80%, carries more weight. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has a Corrigan pulse (a very rapid upstroke followed by rapid collapse) and a de Musset sign (head bobbing with each heartbeat). Which valve lesion is most likely?","choices":["Tricuspid stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Mitral regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Corrigan pulse, also called a water hammer or collapsing pulse, and the de Musset sign are findings of chronic aortic regurgitation, along with a diastolic blowing murmur and a displaced point of maximal impulse. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from diabetes asks what she can do herself to stop fainting when she gets out of bed. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Change position slowly when rising","Stand quickly to train her reflexes","Stay in bed until symptoms stop","Drink less water in the evening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes are listed treatment for orthostatic syncope, with a pause sitting before standing, alongside treatment of the underlying cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman takes sacubitril-valsartan for reduced ejection fraction. Which natriuretic peptide result should not be relied on to judge her congestion?","choices":["Neither peptide, since neither is altered","N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide","B-type natriuretic peptide","Either peptide, since both are altered"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neprilysin degrades B-type natriuretic peptide, so blocking neprilysin with sacubitril alters that level. The N-terminal fragment is the one to rely on. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a fasting low-density lipoprotein of 215 mg/dL on repeat testing. Her thyroid function, kidney function and urinalysis are normal, and she has no atherosclerotic disease. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Lifestyle alone for six months","Moderate-intensity statin after risk calculation","Coronary artery calcium scoring","High-intensity statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein of 190 mg/dL or more is severe primary hypercholesterolemia: begin a high-intensity statin without calculating 10-year risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman presents with acute right hemiparesis and aphasia of two hours' duration. Her blood pressure is 196/104. How should her blood pressure be managed?","choices":["Reduce the mean arterial pressure by a quarter in an hour","Reduce the pressure to below 130/80 immediately","Follow the acute stroke protocol, not chronic targets","Leave the blood pressure entirely untreated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute stroke is specifically excepted from the general emergency reduction approach, because driving the pressure to a chronic target can extend the ischemic injury. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the treatment for a patient with familial hypercholesterolemia refractory to drug therapy?","choices":["Evolocumab or alirocumab","Switch the statin to a fibrate","Low-intensity statin instead","A very low-fat diet alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients refractory to drug therapy may be treated with a proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 inhibitor such as evolocumab or alirocumab, after high-intensity statins with ezetimibe and nicotinic acid. It is not a first step. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with Parkinson disease has neurogenic orthostatic hypotension and supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). Which medication is best suited to her?","choices":["Vasopressin","Midodrine","Phenylephrine","Droxidopa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Droxidopa has mild efficacy but is better with supine hypertension, the setting in which midodrine is avoided. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man is seen in the emergency department after fainting while voiding at night. His examination is normal. Which test excludes hypoglycemia as the cause?","choices":["Orthostatic vital signs","A blood glucose level","A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A normal blood glucose rules out hypoglycemia, a non-hypotensive cause, as blood counts and a metabolic panel address infection and metabolic causes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man had 30 minutes of chest pressure at rest. His electrocardiogram shows nonspecific T-wave changes, and his first troponin I is not abnormal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Discharge him home now","Order a calcium score","Repeat the troponin I","Give a thrombolytic agent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is drawn serially: when one is drawn, a second follows, because the change over time is what matters. A rising troponin I would turn this presentation into an infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"}]};
